## Verify the download

```sh
shasum -a 256 -c desktop-name-manager-@VERSION@-arm64.zip.sha256   # expects: desktop-name-manager-@VERSION@-arm64.zip: OK
ditto -x -k desktop-name-manager-@VERSION@-arm64.zip dnm-@VERSION@
codesign -dvv "dnm-@VERSION@/Desktop Name Manager.app/Contents/Helpers/dnm" 2>&1 | grep -E "Authority=Developer ID|flags=.*runtime"
codesign --verify --deep --strict --verbose=2 "dnm-@VERSION@/Desktop Name Manager.app"
xcrun stapler validate "dnm-@VERSION@/Desktop Name Manager.app"
```

The zip holds `Desktop Name Manager.app` (with `dnm` inside it, in `Contents/Helpers/`), `LICENSE`,
`Acknowledgements.md` (the open-source components, with their licenses), their license texts in `Licenses/`, and the
shell completions in `completions/`; `dnm about` lists the components too.

SHA-256 of `desktop-name-manager-@VERSION@-arm64.zip`: `@SHA256@`. Changing a single byte of the zip makes the checksum
check fail. The app and `dnm` are signed with a Developer ID and the hardened runtime, and the app is notarized by Apple
and stapled: a quarantined copy (as a browser or Homebrew downloads it) runs without a Gatekeeper warning.
